From 9909abbd96861e50f4910cdcbdea0f82d3a9b9f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:42:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] WIP/prototype: event-driven per-peer P2P socket I/O on an
 asio strand

Prototype, not merge-ready. A small io_context thread pool (-p2piothreads,
default 2, 0 = poll loop only) watches each peer's native descriptor on a
per-peer strand. Receive (recv, TLS, WebSocket deframing, zstd,
deserialization into the process queue) and send-queue flushing run on the
peer's strand as soon as the socket is ready, instead of after the socket
thread's next poll (up to 50 ms for peers added while it sleeps). Message
processing is unchanged and stays on the message handler thread, one
message per peer at a time.

The socket thread keeps listening sockets, peers without a real descriptor
(test mocks), disconnection, inactivity checks and inflight throttling.
Readiness waits are armed only after an operation would block, as Asio's
reactors are edge-triggered. NodeIo holds a CNode reference and a Sock
reference until the descriptor is deregistered, so neither the CNode nor
the descriptor number can be released while a handler may run.
